      "text_raw": "For sake of comparison, a\nshort table is given listing\nthe main advantages and\ndisadvantages.\nThe four-transistor single-\nstage voltage differential\namplifier is the first on the\nlist. It is followed by a sym-\nmetrical CMOS OTA. Then\nwe have two cascode CMOS\nOTA’s. Finally, a two-stage\nMiller CMOS OTA is\nadded.\nIt is clear that the Miller\nCMOS OTA takes the high-\nest power consumption. The\nbest one is a telescopic cascode.\nFor high output swing, the telescopic cascode OTA is the worst one. The best are the Miller\nCMOS OTA and the symmetrical one, at least if no cascodes are used!\nThe symmetrical OTA is the worst for noise, however.\nThis shows that even for as few as three specifications, not one single amplifier can be called\nthe best. Many designers prefer a folded cascode OTA, which is certainly a good compromise.",
      "text": "For sake of comparison, a short table is given listing the main advantages and disadvantages. The four-transistor singlestage voltage differential amplifier is the first on the list. It is followed by a symmetrical CMOS OTA. Then we have two cascode CMOS OTA’s. Finally, a two-stage Miller CMOS OTA is added. It is clear that the Miller CMOS OTA takes the highest power consumption. The best one is a telescopic cascode. For high output swing, the telescopic cascode OTA is the worst one. The best are the Miller CMOS OTA and the symmetrical one, at least if no cascodes are used! The symmetrical OTA is the worst for noise, however. This shows that even for as few as three specifications, not one single amplifier can be called the best. Many designers prefer a folded cascode OTA, which is certainly a good compromise."
    }
  ],
  "extraction_status": "source_extracted",
  "semantic_review_status": "unreviewed",
  "commentary_text": "For sake of comparison, a short table is given listing the main advantages and disadvantages. The four-transistor singlestage voltage differential amplifier is the first on the list. It is followed by a symmetrical CMOS OTA. Then we have two cascode CMOS OTA’s. Finally, a two-stage Miller CMOS OTA is added. It is clear that the Miller CMOS OTA takes the highest power consumption. The best one is a telescopic cascode. For high output swing, the telescopic cascode OTA is the worst one. The best are the Miller CMOS OTA and the symmetrical one, at least if no cascodes are used! The symmetrical OTA is the worst for noise, however. This shows that even for as few as three specifications, not one single amplifier can be called the best. Many designers prefer a folded cascode OTA, which is certainly a good compromise.",
  "schema_version": "1.0",
